Title: LAST ROUND
Credit: Story and direction by
Author: Hung Quach
Source: Screenplay draft by Claude (Cine Lab), from choices made by the author at Gates 1 and 2
Draft date: 2026-09-27 — Gate 2, draft 2
Notes: Target running time 2:30 (150 s) before credits. Only IDA speaks (4 lines). CAS has no dialogue. Optics follow bible/world-rules.md v0.2. Marks [[P: ...]] are open choices for the author.

# ACT ONE

= Scene 1 — The Round. Dusk. Ida lights her street as she has every night; her watch is slow. (0:00–0:26, 26 s)

EXT. THE CITY - HIGH ANGLE - DUSK

[[0:00]] High above the rooftops of a nameless city, the sky fading from rose to violet. One by one, small amber points appear along the curving streets — gas lamps, each one a warm centre with dark between.

EXT. OSTLER STREET - DUSK

[[0:04]] A curved street of brick houses. Eleven gas lamps; between them, taller posts with broad frosted panels stand unlit, their wires still new.

IDA MARROW (74), upright, long split-tailed coat, soft felt hat, walks up the street with a short wooden ladder across her right shoulder.

She sets the ladder against a lamp, climbs three rungs, opens the valve, touches in the flame with her lighting pole.

*Phup.* Amber blooms. Her shadow falls long and soft across the cobbles.

She holds both palms to the glass. One. Two. Three. She tips the flame of her pole into the small tin lantern at her belt and snaps its door shut.

[[0:12]]

IDA
(softly, to the lamp)
Evening, old street.

[[0:16]] At the foot of the ladder she takes out a pocket watch, glances at it, taps its glass twice with a fingernail — an old, fond habit — and puts it away.

[[0:20]] She walks on, lamp after lamp: pools of amber, bands of dark between them. Her shadow swings from one side of her to the other as she passes each post.

[[0:24]] Far up the street, in the square, a relay CLICKS.

= Scene 2 — Switch-on. The new lights arrive, starting with a clock; they take her shadow. (0:26–0:44, 18 s)

EXT. OSTLER STREET / THE SQUARE - CONTINUOUS

[[0:26]] In the square at the head of the street, on a tall iron post, a round clock face lights up first: frosted white glass, twelve black bars, two black hands, no numerals. The hands stand at the hour.

A bell on the post strikes once — a clean electric DING.

Then the frosted panels around the square flicker twice and stand white. Then the next block. Then the next. A wave of flat white light rolls down the hill with a low hum.

[[0:34]] Ida, on her ladder at lamp seven, turns.

The white reaches the lamps she lit a minute ago. Their amber pools thin and vanish into an even white. The bands of dark between the posts are gone.

[[0:40]] The wave reaches her. She looks down.

Her long shadow is gone. Only a faint grey smudge under her boots.

She lifts one hand. Nothing moves on the cobbles.

# ACT TWO

= Scene 3 — The Race. She is late for the first time; she hurries to light what is left. (0:44–1:08, 24 s)

EXT. OSTLER STREET - LOWER END - CONTINUOUS

[[0:44]] Ida climbs down and looks back up the hill at the lit clock.

[[0:46]] [[P: open choice 8 — clock beat staging. Draft uses option A.]] She raises her pocket watch at arm's length, so that in her eyeline it sits just below the distant clock. Close on the two faces, one above the other, the near one sharp, then the far one sharp: the white clock's minute hand has just passed the top; hers has not yet reached it. The two hands sit either side of twelve with a clear gap between them — seven minutes.

She lowers the watch. For the first time in forty years, she is late. A dry, small breath of a laugh. She shoulders the ladder and walks fast.

[[0:52]] Lamp eight. Lamp nine. Each one blooms amber and throws her shadow long — and a few seconds later the white overtakes it and the shadow is erased.

[[0:58]] She climbs lamp ten too fast, fumbles the pole, catches it.

IDA
(under her breath)
Not yet... not yet.

The lamp catches. The white arrives. The amber drowns.

[[1:03]] The last lamp, the eleventh, stands at the end of the street by the lime-white wall of a warehouse. Its electric post is still dark: the last block has not switched over yet. She hurries, climbs, lights it. Amber.

She holds her palms to the glass. One. Two. Three. She does not see the boy watching her from the wall.

= Scene 4 — The Wall. A boy is making a bird out of her light. The white takes it. She gives it back. (1:08–1:34, 26 s)

EXT. END OF OSTLER STREET - WAREHOUSE WALL - CONTINUOUS

[[1:08]] Between the eleventh lamp and the warehouse wall, CAS (10), thin, knit cap with a bobble, ears sticking out, faces the wall with both arms raised.

On the wall: a bird. His thumbs locked, his fingers spread, it flaps slowly.

Ida climbs down. Watches. She does not call out.

[[1:16]] The electric post above them clicks, flickers twice, and stands white. White floods the wall.

The bird fades — grey, then nothing. Cas's hands are still up, flapping at a blank wall. He flaps harder. Nothing.

He lowers his hands, turns, and sees Ida — and the small tin lantern at her belt, still glowing amber.

He looks at the lantern. He does not ask.

[[1:26]] Ida unhooks the lantern and crouches beside him, holding it low behind his hands, an arm's length from the wall.

[[1:28]]

IDA
Go on, then.

Cas raises his hands into the little pool of amber. On the wall a bird appears — bigger than before, warm, soft-edged, because the little flame is so close — and flies.

Cas lets out a soft laugh.

# ACT THREE

= Scene 5 — The Last Lamp. Two shadows; then Ida puts out her own last light and gives the flame away. (1:34–2:10, 36 s)

EXT. WAREHOUSE LOADING BAY - CONTINUOUS

[[1:34]] [[P: open choice 7 — shielded wall. Draft uses option A.]] A few steps along the wall, a deep arched loading bay cuts into the warehouse. Inside the arch, the white does not reach: the back wall of the bay is dim, bare, lime-washed.

Ida sets the lantern down on the cobbles a few steps from that back wall, just inside the arch. She and Cas step past it and stand between the lantern and the wall, closer to the wall.

On the back wall, thrown by the little lantern, two shadows rise side by side, large and soft: hers, tall, hat brim and split coat; his, small, bobble cap, hands half-raised. Outside the arch, the whole street is white and has no shadows at all.

[[1:42]] Ida looks at the two shadows for a long moment.

[[1:44]] Then she steps out of the arch. Above the street, the eleventh gas lamp still burns — pale now, a weak yellow thumbprint inside the white. She climbs the ladder to it. Cas follows and holds the ladder with both hands. Her hand rests on the valve.

[[1:50]]

IDA
(softly, voice catching)
That's the last one, then. Goodnight, old street. You'll be brighter now. Just... keep a little dark for the ones who need it.

[[2:04]] She turns the valve. The last gas lamp in the city goes out. Nothing else changes: the white does not even flicker.

[[2:06]] At the foot of the ladder she fetches the lantern from the arch and holds it out to Cas. He takes it with both hands.

[[2:07]] He looks at it. Then, without being told, he holds his palms to the glass, exactly as she does. One. Two. Three.

Ida sees it. She says nothing.

= Scene 6 — The Window. The street is white; one window is not. Ida takes the new time. (2:10–2:30, 20 s)

EXT. THE CITY - HIGH ANGLE - NIGHT

[[2:10]] The same high view as the opening. The whole city an even, sleepless white. In the middle of it, one small window glows amber.

EXT. ALLEY BELOW CAS'S WINDOW - NIGHT

[[2:14]] A narrow alley between two houses, where the electric light from the street does not reach. Above, Cas's window glows amber.

Ida stands in the alley under it. In the window's faint spill, a short, pale shadow of her lies on the cobbles again, leaning away from the wall.

[[2:17]] She takes out her pocket watch. Through the mouth of the alley, above the rooftops, the white clock of the square is visible. She looks from one to the other, turns the crown, and moves the minute hand forward — seven minutes — until it matches. She closes the watch and puts it away. [[P: open choice 9 — staging of the watch reset. Draft uses option A.]]

[[2:22]] She shoulders the ladder and walks out of the alley. At its mouth, her faint shadow thins and is gone in the white.

INT. CAS'S ROOM - NIGHT

[[2:25]] The lantern on the sill. Cas stands between it and the far wall, his back to us, hands raised. On the plaster, a large, soft bird opens its wings and flies across the wall.

[[2:30]]

> FADE OUT.

> END
